# B++ will start with very low-level types:
# - byte, the smallest addressable unit of memory
# - word, an unsinged integer of width equal to the integer register size
# - addr, a pointer (effectively still just an unsigned integer) of width large enough to address the whole address space
#
# on a modern desktop/server, a byte will be 8 bits, and word and address will be the same size

main() :: Word
main() {
  42
}

other() {
  67
}
